---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {funktorSidebar} from '../../../data/funktor-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout title="REST Module" sidebar={funktorSidebar} description="Typed API routes with authorization DSL">

    <h1>REST</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        Type-safe API routes, authorization DSL, endpoint documentation, and SSE support.
    </p>

    <h2>Typed API routes</h2>

    <p>Every route carries its parameter types, request body type, and response type at compile time.
        Four route variants cover all REST patterns:</p>

    <div class="not-prose my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <th class="py-2 text-gray-400">Variant</th>
                <th class="py-2 text-gray-400">Use case</th>
            </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <td class="py-2 text-white"><code>ApiRoute.Plain</code></td>
                <td class="py-2 text-gray-400">GET without parameters</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <td class="py-2 text-white"><code>ApiRoute.WithParams</code></td>
                <td class="py-2 text-gray-400">GET/DELETE with path or query parameters</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <td class="py-2 text-white"><code>ApiRoute.WithBody</code></td>
                <td class="py-2 text-gray-400">POST/PUT with a request body</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <td class="py-2 text-white"><code>ApiRoute.WithBodyAndParams</code></td>
                <td class="py-2 text-gray-400">POST/PUT with both params and body</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <Code code={`// Define endpoints in shared code (client + server)
companion object {
    val GetUsers = TypedApiEndpoint.Get(
        uri = "/api/users",
        response = UserModel.serializer().apiList(),
    )
    val CreateUser = TypedApiEndpoint.Post(
        uri = "/api/users",
        body = CreateUserRequest.serializer(),
        response = UserModel.serializer().api(),
    )
}

// Mount on the server with handler, docs, and auth
val getUsers = GetUsers.mount {
    docs { name = "List users" }
    .codeGen { funcName = "getUsers" }
    .authorize { public() }
    .handle {
        ApiResponse.ok(userService.listAll())
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Authentication pipeline</h2>

    <p>Funktor ships three Ktor auth providers that together produce a typed
        <code>Caller</code> principal for every request. Install them in
        <code>authentication &#123; &#125;</code> and list them on the route's
        <code>authenticate(...)</code> block in the same order:</p>

    <Code code={`authentication {
    jwtCaller(AUTH_JWT, realm = "MyApp")             // Caller.JwtCaller   on a valid JWT
    apiKeyCaller(AUTH_API_KEY, realm = "MyApp") {    // Caller.ApiKeyCaller on a valid key
        token -> kontainer.get<ApiKeyService>().tryResolve(token)
    }
    anonymous(AUTH_ANON)                             // Caller.AnonymousCaller — terminal fallback
}

routing {
    authenticate(AUTH_JWT, AUTH_API_KEY, AUTH_ANON) {
        // ...routes
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Order matters &mdash; under Ktor's default <code>FirstSuccessful</code> strategy, the first
        provider that returns a principal wins. <code>anonymous()</code> always succeeds, so it
        <strong>must be listed last</strong>. Invalid credentials (expired JWT, revoked key) silently
        fall through to <code>AnonymousCaller</code> rather than 401-ing &mdash; routes that require
        real auth check <code>User.isAnonymous()</code>.</p>

    <h3>UserRecord variants</h3>

    <p><code>currentUserProvider()</code> dispatches on the <code>Caller</code> and produces a
        sealed <code>UserRecord</code>:</p>

    <Code code={`when (record) {
    is UserRecord.Anonymous -> // no credentials
    is UserRecord.System    -> // internal system actor (background jobs)
    is UserRecord.LoggedIn  -> // JWT-authenticated end-user
    is UserRecord.ApiKey    -> // API-key authenticated; carries keyId + keyName for audit
}

// Wire the provider into the per-call kontainer
installKontainer { call ->
    app.kontainers.create {
        with { call.currentUserProvider() }
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>The default <code>jwtCaller</code> validates the bearer token via the kontainer-bound
        <code>JwtGenerator</code>. Pass a custom <code>validate</code> lambda when you need extra
        checks (revocation list, audience switching, multi-tenant verifier selection):</p>

    <Code code={`jwtCaller(AUTH_JWT) { token ->
    val payload = jwtGenerator.tryVerify(token) ?: return@jwtCaller null
    if (kontainer.get<RevocationList>().isRevoked(payload.subject)) return@jwtCaller null
    Caller.JwtCaller(payload)
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <Aside><strong>API-key resolver checklist:</strong> hashed storage (Argon2/HMAC, never plaintext),
        constant-time comparison via <code>MessageDigest.isEqual</code>, fast-reject by prefix before
        any DB lookup, never log the raw token. The <code>permissions</code> on
        <code>Caller.ApiKeyCaller</code> defaults to <code>UserPermissions.anonymous</code> &mdash;
        derive privileges from your authoritative store, never from the caller's input.
    </Aside>

    <h2>The authorization floor</h2>

    <p>Every <code>ApiRoutes</code> group must declare an <code>authFloor</code>. It has no default, so a
        group cannot be written without deciding who may reach it &mdash; the floor is prepended to every
        route in the group, and it is append-only: a route can add restrictions, never remove them.</p>

    <Code code={`class OrgsApi : ApiRoutes("orgs", authFloor = { isSuperUser() }) {
    val list = ApiRoute.Get("", ...)
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Floors are materialized and validated at group construction, so a malformed one aborts app start
        rather than surfacing as a hole at runtime.</p>

    <h2>Authorization DSL</h2>

    <p>Rules are <strong>statements, not values</strong>. Each call appends to the chain, and the chain is
        an AND. There is no <code>and</code>/<code>or</code> infix &mdash; to express alternatives, nest a
        block combinator.</p>

    <Code code={`.authorize {
    // The chain is an AND — both must hold
    authenticated()
    forPermission("orders.read")

    // Alternatives: nest forAny {} (OR) or forAll {} (AND)
    forAny {
        forRole("admin")
        forGroup("staff")
    }

    // Custom rule
    forCall("owns resource") { params ->
        user.id == params.ownerId
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <div class="not-prose my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">Rule</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">Passes for</th>
            </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-400">
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>authenticated()</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">any non-anonymous caller</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>isSuperUser()</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">super users</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>forUserType(t)</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">callers of that user type</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>forRole(r)</code> / <code>forAnyRole(…)</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">role holders</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>forGroup(g)</code> / <code>forAnyGroup(…)</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">group members</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>forPermission(p)</code> / <code>forAnyPermission(…)</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">permission holders</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>forCall(desc) { }</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">a custom predicate over params/body</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>public()</code> / <code>forbidden()</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">everyone / nobody &mdash; see below</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p><strong><code>public()</code> and <code>forbidden()</code> must be the SOLE rule of a chain.</strong>
        Combined with anything else they silently become a no-op or an always-allow, so the builder rejects
        it at boot. To serve both a public and a protected audience, split them into separate
        <code>ApiRoutes</code> groups, each with its own floor. An empty <code>forAll {}</code> /
        <code>forAny {}</code> is rejected for the same reason &mdash; an empty AND allows everyone and an
        empty OR denies everyone.</p>

    <Aside>The statements-only shape is deliberate. An expression DSL made the last expression silently win,
        which reads as a tightening and evaluates as a replacement &mdash; the kind of bug that looks correct
        in review and is wide open in production.
    </Aside>

    <Aside>Authorization rules are also used for access estimation &mdash; the admin UI can grey out actions
        a user cannot perform, without making the actual API call.
    </Aside>

    <h2>SSE routes</h2>

    <p>Server-Sent Events routes stream data from server to client. Same type-safe pattern,
        but the handler receives a <code>ServerSSESession</code>.</p>

    <Code code={`val SseEvents = TypedApiEndpoint.Sse(uri = "/api/events")

val sseEvents = SseEvents.mount(Unit::class) {
    authorize { public() }
    .handle {
        while (true) {
            send(ServerSentEvent(data = Json.encodeToString(payload), event = "update"))
            delay(1000)
        }
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>API features</h2>

    <p>Group related routes into <code>ApiFeature</code> implementations. Register them in Kontainer and they
        are auto-discovered and mounted.</p>

    <Code code={`class UserApiFeature : ApiFeature {
    override val name = "Users"
    override val description = "User management endpoints"

    val users = UserApi()
    val profiles = ProfileApi()

    override fun getRouteGroups() = listOf(users, profiles)
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <div class="mt-12">
        <a href="/ultra/funktor/auth"
           class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-lg bg-brand-400/10 text-brand-400 border border-brand-400/30 hover:bg-brand-400/20 transition-all font-medium">
            Auth Module &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
